/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : N-Queens II
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Backtracking
Difficulty : Hard

Problem:
Given an integer n, return the number of distinct ways to
place n queens on an n x n chessboard such that no two queens
attack each other.

Approach:
Use Backtracking to place exactly one queen in each row.

For every row, try each column and check whether the position
is safe using three boolean arrays:
- `columns[col]` tracks occupied columns.
- `mainDiagonal[row - col + n - 1]` tracks main diagonals.
- `antiDiagonal[row + col]` tracks anti-diagonals.

If the position is safe, place the queen, mark the occupied
column and diagonals, and recursively process the next row.

After returning from recursion, undo the placement so that
the next possible position can be explored.

Unlike N-Queens I, we do not need to store the board because
only the total number of valid configurations is required.

Why does this work?

Each valid solution places one queen in every row without
sharing a column or diagonal with another queen.

The tracking arrays allow us to check whether a position is
safe in O(1) time. Backtracking explores every valid placement
and increments the answer whenever all n rows are successfully
filled.

Algorithm:
1. Initialize arrays for columns and both diagonal directions.
2. Start backtracking from row 0.
3. Try every column in the current row.
4. Skip the position if its column or diagonals are occupied.
5. Place the queen and mark the corresponding constraints.
6. Recursively process the next row.
7. Undo the placement after recursion.
8. When all rows are filled, increment the solution count.
9. Return the total number of solutions.

Time Complexity : O(n!)

Reason:
The backtracking process explores the possible queen
placements, with O(1) constraint checking for each position.

Space Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
The column and diagonal tracking arrays require O(n) space,
and the recursion depth is O(n).

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
private:

    // Counts all valid N-Queens configurations.
    void backtrack(
        int row,
        int n,
        vector<bool>& columns,
        vector<bool>& mainDiagonal,
        vector<bool>& antiDiagonal,
        int& solutionCount
    ) {

        // All queens have been placed successfully.
        if (row == n) {
            solutionCount++;
            return;
        }

        // Try every column in the current row.
        for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {

            int mainDiagonalIndex = row - col + n - 1;
            int antiDiagonalIndex = row + col;

            // Skip positions attacked by another queen.
            if (columns[col] ||
                mainDiagonal[mainDiagonalIndex] ||
                antiDiagonal[antiDiagonalIndex]) {
                continue;
            }

            // Place the queen and mark the constraints.
            columns[col] = true;
            mainDiagonal[mainDiagonalIndex] = true;
            antiDiagonal[antiDiagonalIndex] = true;

            // Continue placing queens in the next row.
            backtrack(
                row + 1,
                n,
                columns,
                mainDiagonal,
                antiDiagonal,
                solutionCount
            );

            // Undo the placement.
            columns[col] = false;
            mainDiagonal[mainDiagonalIndex] = false;
            antiDiagonal[antiDiagonalIndex] = false;
        }
    }

public:

    int totalNQueens(int n) {

        // Track occupied columns and diagonals.
        vector<bool> columns(n, false);
        vector<bool> mainDiagonal(2 * n - 1, false);
        vector<bool> antiDiagonal(2 * n - 1, false);

        int solutionCount = 0;

        // Start placing queens from the first row.
        backtrack(
            0,
            n,
            columns,
            mainDiagonal,
            antiDiagonal,
            solutionCount
        );

        return solutionCount;
    }
};
